    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate focusing heavily on attacking favorable pitching matchups to maximize production. **James Wood** (78.42) anchors our outfield, serving as the strongest play of the day; he benefits from a premier matchup against Rodriguez, whose 4.84 xERA suggests a volatile outing, and his elite 0.426 xwOBA combined with a stolen base green light against a slow delivery makes him a locked-in must-start. **Austin Riley** (74.11) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (73.70) follow closely, with **Austin Riley** enjoying a massive +16% park adjustment that elevates his power potential against Rogers, while **Tyler Soderstrom** capitalizes on the platoon advantage against Matthews. **Brice Turang** (71.09) rounds out our top-tier targets, as his high-order placement and a significant +19% boost from the matchup against Sugano\u2019s 5.12 xERA make him our primary engine for Runs and RBI today.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Zach McKinstry**, who narrowly missed the cut at a 42.67 efficiency score. While his park factor is favorable, his lower base projection and sub-optimal batting order in the #7 hole relegated him to the bench in favor of higher-floor options like **Henry Bolte**, whose inclusion despite a lower order placement remains a calculated gamble on potential counting stats. We are also proceeding with caution regarding **Edwin Arroyo**; despite being a bench option, he carries a 41% rain risk, and we\u2019ve opted to keep him sidelined to avoid the variance of a potential weather-shortened performance. Meanwhile, starters like **Jose Caballero** and **Seiya Suzuki** face tougher sledding against Luzardo and Jones, respectively; both deal with negative SP skill adjustments, but **Jose Caballero** remains in the lineup specifically for his stolen base utility, as our strategy heavily weights speed in tight 5x5 Roto races.",
